WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.086
Hello, everyone!

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:05.611
Welcome to the channel "Mathematical Experiments."

00:00:05.611 --> 00:00:10.288
Today, we have another dose of experimental mathematics without proofs.

00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:13.788
There's proven medicine, proven mathematics,

00:00:13.788 --> 00:00:16.712
and then there's our unproven mathematics.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:21.202
By the way, our unproven mathematics is very close to proven medicine.

00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:24.139
We conduct experiments.

00:00:24.139 --> 00:00:26.991
We get results and observe them.

00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:30.655
And we consider that to be the most important proof.

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:35.342
Of course, it's not the most significant proof. It would be great to have a real proof.

00:00:35.342 --> 00:00:40.185
But you should seek it on your own.

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:47.051
Here, we don't consider the fact that we lack a genuine proof as an obstacle.

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:49.488
We simply discover interesting facts.

00:00:49.488 --> 00:00:51.873
Last time, we discovered an interesting fact

00:00:51.873 --> 00:00:57.692
that the sum of the k-th powers of the first "n" natural numbers can be expressed as a polynomial.

00:00:57.692 --> 00:01:03.413
In Mathematica, there is an answer to the question of how to calculate the sum of the k-th powers of the first "n" natural numbers.

00:01:03.413 --> 00:01:06.667
It's called the HarmonicNumber function.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.993
In Mathematica, there are also Bernoulli polynomials

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:13.345
that allow us to get the same result using a different method.

00:01:13.345 --> 00:01:21.351
If you want to sum the k-th powers from 1 to 10, you need to take the value of the Bernoulli polynomial at point 11,

00:01:21.351 --> 00:01:24.509
subtract the value of the Bernoulli polynomial at point 0,

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:27.205
divide by k + 1,

00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:29.748
and the first argument is also equal to k + 1.

00:01:29.748 --> 00:01:36.086
That's what you need to do to get the sum of squares of the first ten natural numbers.

00:01:36.086 --> 00:01:43.460
A concise expression in the Mathematica language to explicitly define it.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:49.345
HarmonicNumber. This function is defined in Mathematica as follows.

00:01:49.345 --> 00:01:55.570
To calculate the sum of the first ten natural numbers raised to the power of 3,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.157
you can perform these calculations.

00:01:57.157 --> 00:01:59.669
They all yield the same result.

00:01:59.669 --> 00:02:00.708
Okay.

00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:06.049
But we won't rely on the achievements of humanity in the form of a pre-defined HarmonicNumber function.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:13.525
We will define this HarmonicNumber ourselves like this.

00:02:13.525 --> 00:02:17.001
"Nk" is a function that returns a list of k-th powers.

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:20.805
Here, we got the squares of the first ten numbers.

00:02:20.805 --> 00:02:23.626
"AccNk" is very straightforward.

00:02:23.626 --> 00:02:27.789
Each number is the sum of powers.

00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:30.190
For example, 5 is the sum of 1 + 4.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:35.520
14 is the sum of 1 + 4 + 9, and so on.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.000
So, the array returned by AccNk

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.924
is the HarmonicNumber for different values of the first argument "m".

00:02:48.924 --> 00:02:50.369
Okay.

00:02:50.369 --> 00:02:56.802
AccNk is translated as "Accumulate list N to the power of k."

00:02:56.802 --> 00:02:59.780
You could get AccNk like this.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.710
And perhaps it would calculate faster...

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.041
but we won't. We'll do everything in a simple way

00:03:05.041 --> 00:03:13.438
so that what we're doing right now can be replicated in C++, Python, and so on.

00:03:13.438 --> 00:03:16.811
Let's plot our list.

00:03:16.811 --> 00:03:23.073
For example, this is what we get when we sum the cubes.

00:03:23.073 --> 00:03:26.100
It's a strongly growing function.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.703
Today, I'd like to show you

00:03:27.703 --> 00:03:34.521
how you can get the same Bernoulli polynomials with their tricky coefficients

00:03:34.521 --> 00:03:39.296
without using a system of linear equations.

00:03:39.296 --> 00:03:43.522
Without using what's called matrix inversion,

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:46.589
but instead using programming skills

00:03:46.589 --> 00:03:55.291
so that a programmer can quickly get all these tricky coefficients of the Bernoulli polynomials.

00:03:55.291 --> 00:03:57.583
How will we do it?

00:03:57.583 --> 00:04:00.302
The programmer sees a growing function.

00:04:00.302 --> 00:04:05.153
He has a desire to know how it grows.

00:04:05.153 --> 00:04:07.350
And here we can experiment.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:13.410
Let's divide, for example, by the square - it still grows.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.477
Divide by cubes - it still grows.

00:04:16.477 --> 00:04:20.000
Divide by four - oops... it doesn't grow anymore.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.306
It's evident that it tends. And it tends not to zero, but to some number.

00:04:27.306 --> 00:04:30.660
What number does it tend to?

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:39.829
Let's try to repeat this experiment for k = 4. The sum of fourth powers.

00:04:39.829 --> 00:04:43.066
We also find that the function grows

00:04:43.066 --> 00:04:53.347
and when we divide by the k-th power of "n", we get this converging thing.

00:04:53.347 --> 00:05:00.000
Those who know calculus already guess the answer here. What does this line tend to?

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.888
Here it tends to 1/5, which is 0.2.

00:05:03.888 --> 00:05:08.512
And above, it tends to 0.25.

00:05:08.512 --> 00:05:11.598
Why does this happen?

00:05:11.598 --> 00:05:12.803
Let's understand it with the square.

00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:15.546
Here's our parabola.

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:19.333
When we sum the squares, it means

00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:22.357
that we sum these columns.

00:05:22.357 --> 00:05:28.609
We want to calculate the area of this tan color.

00:05:28.609 --> 00:05:34.079
Each column has an area equal to n^2.

00:05:34.079 --> 00:05:36.415
And so, when we add up the area of all the columns,

00:05:36.415 --> 00:05:42.384
we precisely get the sum of squares of the first "n" natural numbers.

00:05:42.384 --> 00:05:47.202
But look, there's a cool thing here.

00:05:47.202 --> 00:05:51.878
Those who know calculus,

00:05:51.878 --> 00:05:55.186
they know what this blue area is equal to.

00:05:55.186 --> 00:06:05.531
It's easier to compute than the tan one, strangely enough.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:10.000
The process of calculating the area under the graph is called integration.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:20.000
And the integral, for example, for a parabola is equal to (m^3)/3 when we sum up to "m".

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.384
It's evident that the blue area...

00:06:22.384 --> 00:06:26.134
The entire blue area. Not just the upper triangles, but the entire blue area.

00:06:26.134 --> 00:06:29.031
...and the tan area, they are close.

00:06:29.031 --> 00:06:38.325
And that's why most likely this tan area grows with the same strength as the blue one.

00:06:38.325 --> 00:06:39.759
So...

00:06:41.082 --> 00:06:50.213
Therefore, when we sum the squares, we need to divide by the cube, by three.

00:06:50.213 --> 00:06:55.024
Here, I want to divide "m^3" by 3.

00:06:55.024 --> 00:07:00.817
Just that the three ended up here when I started dividing.

00:07:00.817 --> 00:07:06.952
This expression in parentheses is (m^3)/3.

00:07:06.952 --> 00:07:09.271
We just need to remove it here.

00:07:09.271 --> 00:07:12.831
And if we sum the cubes,

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:16.533
we need to write a 4 here.

00:07:16.533 --> 00:07:22.027
And the tendency will also be towards one. And so on.

00:07:25.015 --> 00:07:38.464
The fact that the blue and tan areas coincide asymptotically can be seen from the image.

00:07:38.464 --> 00:07:40.213
Slap! See?

00:07:40.213 --> 00:07:45.297
You can't see this little difference in blue.

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:50.627
This means that it is negligible compared to the entire brown area.

00:07:50.627 --> 00:08:01.789
The difference between the total blue and total tan areas is negligible compared to their sizes.

00:08:01.789 --> 00:08:02.994
Okay.

00:08:02.994 --> 00:08:05.372
Moving on.

00:08:05.372 --> 00:08:10.482
Next, we want to calculate more.

00:08:10.482 --> 00:08:15.352
Let's move the 4 here after all.

00:08:15.352 --> 00:08:19.805
Let's work with the 3 for now.

00:08:19.805 --> 00:08:22.317
There you go. Let's start with the 3.

00:08:22.317 --> 00:08:23.847
We get 1.

00:08:23.847 --> 00:08:25.969
Subtract this 1.

00:08:25.969 --> 00:08:31.506
We get 1 at infinity on the right.

00:08:31.506 --> 00:08:36.088
We get a graph that keeps getting smaller and smaller...

00:08:36.088 --> 00:08:44.912
Let's enclose this in parentheses and multiply it by, let's say, "m".

00:08:44.912 --> 00:08:48.761
This expression here represents "m" for me.

00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:50.670
Slap.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:55.077
Degree 1 is "m," and when I write 4 here, it will be n^4.

00:08:55.077 --> 00:09:04.167
For now, I just want to multiply by "m".

00:09:04.167 --> 00:09:08.552
Got the answer.

00:09:08.552 --> 00:09:10.442
Something approaching 2.

00:09:10.442 --> 00:09:11.497
Well, okay...

00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:13.881
Subtracting 2.

00:09:13.881 --> 00:09:23.217
Enclose everything in parentheses again and multiply by a linearly increasing thing.

00:09:23.217 --> 00:09:23.973
Oh!

00:09:23.973 --> 00:09:25.418
I get 1.

00:09:25.418 --> 00:09:29.339
Subtracting 1.

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:31.489
So what did I get?

00:09:31.489 --> 00:09:36.250
I got the following expression,

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.573
which equals 0.

00:09:39.573 --> 00:09:49.333
Let's denote this as HarmonicNumber[3].

00:09:49.333 --> 00:09:56.066
I'll call this m^4.

00:09:56.066 --> 00:09:58.530
I'll simply call this "m".

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.317
This is also "m".

00:10:00.317 --> 00:10:05.854
One parenthesis got lost... There we go.

00:10:05.854 --> 00:10:15.223
And I want to solve this equation with respect to this thing here.

00:10:15.223 --> 00:10:20.269
Here's what I got.

00:10:20.269 --> 00:10:25.670
So... Expand...

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:27.352
It matches.

00:10:27.352 --> 00:10:34.634
What has just been demonstrated is a way to get HarmonicNumber for k = 3,

00:10:34.634 --> 00:10:38.982
well, or -3 if we're talking about the argument of this function.

00:10:38.982 --> 00:10:44.599
Similarly, we can get other HarmonicNumbers,

00:10:44.599 --> 00:10:51.215
which are slightly transformed Bernoulli polynomials.

00:10:51.215 --> 00:10:53.062
Let's move on.

00:10:53.062 --> 00:10:57.320
Let's calculate for k = 4...

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:57.964
Alright.

00:10:57.964 --> 00:11:02.297
I'll handle this differently here...

00:11:02.297 --> 00:11:10.805
Divide by Nk to the power of 5.

00:11:10.805 --> 00:11:15.061
Well, and I'll write 5 explicitly here too.

00:11:15.061 --> 00:11:15.561
Okay...

00:11:15.561 --> 00:11:18.964
Everything tends to 1, as we expected.

00:11:18.964 --> 00:11:21.705
Subtract 1,

00:11:21.705 --> 00:11:23.364
enclose in parentheses,

00:11:23.364 --> 00:11:29.226
and multiply by what we call "m."

00:11:29.226 --> 00:11:31.226
Slap!

00:11:32.293 --> 00:11:33.807
Very close to 2,5.

00:11:33.807 --> 00:11:36.965
Subtract 5/2.

00:11:36.965 --> 00:11:40.636
Enclose everything in parentheses,

00:11:40.636 --> 00:11:48.057
and multiply by "m."

00:11:48.057 --> 00:11:49.563
So...

00:11:49.563 --> 00:11:53.431
1,66666...

00:11:53.431 --> 00:11:57.303
Subtract 1,6666...

00:11:57.303 --> 00:12:12.658
Well, by the way, what is 1.6666...? It's 5/3.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:25.348
Multiply by "m"...

00:12:27.348 --> 00:12:29.818
Apparently, we didn't multiply enough.

00:12:29.818 --> 00:12:32.031
There we go! That's much better!

00:12:32.031 --> 00:12:39.068
See? Sometimes you have to multiply not by "m," but by "m^2" right away.

00:12:39.068 --> 00:12:42.647
Nk[n, 2] is m^2.

00:12:42.647 --> 00:12:50.000
Here's a certain number...

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:54.476
0,15.

00:12:54.476 --> 00:12:56.857
Not strong enough, we need to add more.

00:12:56.857 --> 00:13:01.765
Adding 0,015 gives us 0,165.

00:13:01.765 --> 00:13:07.951
And we need to add more. Now it's 0,0015.

00:13:07.951 --> 00:13:10.471
This is what we get.

00:13:10.471 --> 00:13:13.378
Well, you can already guess what to add next.

00:13:13.378 --> 00:13:19.765
And 0.16666... is precisely 1/6.

00:13:19.765 --> 00:13:21.889
That's it. We have the answer.

00:13:21.889 --> 00:13:24.225
Here's the answer.

00:13:24.225 --> 00:13:31.373
This thing equals zero.

00:13:31.373 --> 00:13:38.178
And here, I want to show you how Mathematica works. Let me demonstrate.

00:13:38.178 --> 00:13:41.595
We can add some letters here,

00:13:41.595 --> 00:13:48.885
enclose everything in parentheses, and transform it using rules.

00:13:48.885 --> 00:13:58.498
We can say that all of this equals HN[k].

00:13:58.498 --> 00:14:03.352
And this...

00:14:03.352 --> 00:14:20.000
place "l_" here and turn all of this into m^l.

00:14:25.251 --> 00:14:26.600
There we go...

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:35.894
Well, write "Solve" with respect to...

00:14:35.894 --> 00:14:42.903
And based on our previous experience, we need to write "Expand," in case it factors it again.

00:14:42.903 --> 00:14:47.191
Let's check...

00:14:51.289 --> 00:14:53.083
We got it.

00:14:53.083 --> 00:14:59.243
This way, you can find polynomials that correspond to HarmonicNumbers

00:14:59.243 --> 00:15:03.414
and then get Bernoulli polynomials as well.

00:15:03.414 --> 00:15:04.768
Here, this was done.

00:15:04.768 --> 00:15:07.266
Slightly differently, I subtracted here at the beginning.

00:15:07.266 --> 00:15:11.395
Instead of dividing right away, I subtracted right away because I knew the answer.

00:15:11.395 --> 00:15:15.842
The same thing was done for five as well.

00:15:15.842 --> 00:15:20.186
And here, it's convenient to define a function called difNK,

00:15:20.186 --> 00:15:28.718
which subtracts the k+1 degree from the sum of the kth powers multiplied by k+1.

00:15:28.718 --> 00:15:42.144
From the tan area multiplied by k+1 subtracts the blue area multiplied by k+1.

00:15:42.144 --> 00:15:45.504
To deal with these triangles,

00:15:45.504 --> 00:15:50.749
which are located between these two areas.

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:59.530
Here, all of this was done for six.

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:01.610
And when we do this for six, what do we notice?

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:05.957
We notice that these numbers appear, 7/2.

00:16:05.957 --> 00:16:12.396
In fact, we should have divided everything by 7, and here I divided by 7.

00:16:12.396 --> 00:16:16.324
I realized that I made a mistake. I shouldn't have written it like this,

00:16:16.324 --> 00:16:19.519
but rather like this.

00:16:21.781 --> 00:16:24.265
Like this... More directly. In explicit form.

00:16:24.265 --> 00:16:27.540
Subtract the blue area from the brown area.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.952
So, this example is erroneous. Let's erase it.

00:16:31.952 --> 00:16:33.306
It's more convenient to work in this format.

00:16:33.306 --> 00:16:36.800
Here, I won't divide by 7 anymore.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:42.843
We've determined...

00:16:42.843 --> 00:16:49.673
And these coefficients, -1/42, 1/6, -1/2, -1/2,

00:16:49.673 --> 00:16:52.868
they're already familiar to you from the previous video.

00:16:52.868 --> 00:16:55.289
In the previous video, we performed this operation.

00:16:55.289 --> 00:17:00.955
We constructed a triangular matrix from binomial coefficients,

00:17:00.955 --> 00:17:03.549
removed its right slope,

00:17:03.549 --> 00:17:08.873
took the inverse matrix, and got this answer.

00:17:08.873 --> 00:17:10.372
And...

00:17:13.061 --> 00:17:20.261
And in this answer, we need to look at this row and see here:

00:17:20.261 --> 00:17:23.761
1/42, -1/42

00:17:23.761 --> 00:17:27.890
-1/6, +1/6

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:33.341
1/2, -1/2

00:17:33.341 --> 00:17:38.357
-1/2, also -1/2

00:17:38.357 --> 00:17:45.053
See? The signs are strictly opposite, except for this last -1/2.

00:17:45.053 --> 00:17:51.709
This is something I always overlook and forget to tell you about.

00:17:51.709 --> 00:17:59.732
When we sum the first "n" k-th powers,

00:17:59.732 --> 00:18:05.034
we get Bernoulli polynomials of m+1, not m.

00:18:05.034 --> 00:18:11.696
What's the difference between the Bernoulli polynomial for m and the Bernoulli polynomial for m+1 (both divided by k)?

00:18:11.696 --> 00:18:14.851
It differs only in one place. Right here.

00:18:14.851 --> 00:18:16.424
And why is that?

00:18:16.424 --> 00:18:24.784
Because the Bernoulli polynomial for n+1 differs from the previous one by exactly m^k.

00:18:24.784 --> 00:18:29.721
Here, k = 6, and we need to substitute k+1.

00:18:29.721 --> 00:18:31.952
And when we add m^6,

00:18:31.952 --> 00:18:37.617
-(m^6)/2 turns into (m^6)/2.

00:18:37.617 --> 00:18:44.264
In the previous video, I consistently ignored this difference and made mistakes...

00:18:44.264 --> 00:18:49.615
I looked. The coefficients match, but I often missed this subtle difference in signs.

00:18:49.615 --> 00:18:55.632
For 7...

00:19:08.395 --> 00:19:15.811
So, in the matrix we drew last time, it contains the answer to the question:

00:19:15.811 --> 00:19:22.863
how should we arrange the numbers in this expression to eventually get 0?

00:19:24.016 --> 00:19:26.015
Good.

00:19:26.015 --> 00:19:28.766
Now let's move on.

00:19:28.766 --> 00:19:34.474
Last time we got a matrix, and it contains the answer for summing k-th powers, where "k" is an integer.

00:19:34.474 --> 00:19:38.944
But what if k = 1/2?

00:19:38.944 --> 00:19:46.643
To study this case, we'll have to complicate the definition of NK and AccNK a bit.

00:19:46.643 --> 00:19:53.290
Specifically, we need to add precision everywhere.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:55.119
Defined it a bit strangely here...

00:19:55.119 --> 00:19:57.669
And I'll have to sum a lot more numbers.

00:19:57.669 --> 00:20:00.000
I'll be summing up to a million.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.738
But plotting a graph with a million points is difficult, so I want to plot only a fraction of these points.

00:20:04.738 --> 00:20:15.867
To take every hundredth number from an array, you can add this thing to the end of the array.

00:20:15.867 --> 00:20:21.346
And specify 100 or any number you want as the "step".

00:20:21.346 --> 00:20:22.952
Example.

00:20:22.952 --> 00:20:29.403
This one if you want to take every second element from the list.

00:20:29.403 --> 00:20:32.578
You can specify from which to which.

00:20:32.578 --> 00:20:37.591
If you don't specify, it takes the first and the last, respectively.

00:20:41.134 --> 00:20:47.106
Enk differs from Nk in that the numbers, which are got there, have high precision.

00:20:47.106 --> 00:20:55.830
I specified a large precision, and it will perform all operations with high precision.

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:56.933
Good.

00:20:56.933 --> 00:21:01.146
So, our "n" has increased.

00:21:01.146 --> 00:21:04.031
Oops. Well, that's too much.

00:21:04.031 --> 00:21:08.507
Let's do what is written here.

00:21:08.507 --> 00:21:10.507
I forgot to remove the answer...

00:21:18.148 --> 00:21:24.395
Below, we have the answer to the question, but let's try not to peek at it.

00:21:24.395 --> 00:21:30.316
For now, let's choose these values and draw a plot.

00:21:30.316 --> 00:21:32.450
It will be computed for a while.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:35.883
I added caching to all these expressions.

00:21:35.883 --> 00:21:42.549
It looks quite strange, probably for C or Python programmers.

00:21:42.549 --> 00:21:46.773
The function is defined as "Function ="

00:21:46.773 --> 00:21:48.916
What's happening here?

00:21:48.916 --> 00:21:51.812
This should be read as follows:

00:21:51.812 --> 00:21:54.916
evaluate the expression...

00:21:54.916 --> 00:21:57.700
So, the function is defined like this. This is its body.

00:21:57.700 --> 00:22:00.294
We need to evaluate this expression,

00:22:00.294 --> 00:22:08.246
store it in the dictionary ENk with the key [n, k],

00:22:08.246 --> 00:22:12.925
and return it as the result as well.

00:22:12.925 --> 00:22:20.477
When calling "ENk," Mathematica first checks if there is a dictionary with that name and the given argument values.

00:22:20.477 --> 00:22:23.727
If there is an answer in the "k -&gt; value" table, it returns it.

00:22:23.727 --> 00:22:31.959
If not, it proceeds to this definition, which computes and stores the final answer in the dictionary.

00:22:31.959 --> 00:22:39.186
This is called "memoization." This is how it is implemented in Mathematica.

00:22:39.186 --> 00:22:44.013
Simply append the expression without an underscore at the end.

00:22:44.013 --> 00:22:46.568
And use the ordinary "=" sign instead of ":=".

00:22:46.568 --> 00:22:50.000
The ordinary "=" sign means "store in the dictionary."

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:54.817
Let's call it that way. As if we simply stored it in the dictionary.

00:22:54.817 --> 00:22:58.918
During this time, it calculated the growing graph.

00:22:58.918 --> 00:23:04.369
And I suspect it approaches the point 1/2.

00:23:04.369 --> 00:23:08.071
Great. We had 1/2 there, and here it also becomes 1/2,

00:23:08.071 --> 00:23:11.864
even though we are summing not integer powers but roots.

00:23:11.864 --> 00:23:17.999
We are currently calculating the sum of roots of the first "n" natural numbers.

00:23:17.999 --> 00:23:20.287
We subtracted.

00:23:20.287 --> 00:23:23.358
We got a number that now approaches zero.

00:23:23.358 --> 00:23:28.090
What should we multiply it by?

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:33.359
Well, let's multiply it by this...

00:23:33.359 --> 00:23:36.569
Of course, we could multiply by one out of habit,

00:23:36.569 --> 00:23:45.570
but here we see that the graph grows sharply modulo.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:53.717
That is, when summing roots, we should multiply here not by "m" but by "√m".

00:23:53.717 --> 00:23:55.241
Next.

00:23:55.241 --> 00:24:00.000
This number... it's unclear what it is.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.875
Let's add it.

00:24:01.875 --> 00:24:08.436
+0.20784

00:24:08.436 --> 00:24:13.382
I wrote down exactly what I see here.

00:24:13.382 --> 00:24:15.095
So...

00:24:15.095 --> 00:24:23.833
Here, we probably need to add a two...

00:24:23.833 --> 00:24:25.833
More than a two.

00:24:25.833 --> 00:24:28.567
Probably an eight.

00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:29.484
Well, never mind...

00:24:29.484 --> 00:24:33.783
That means we won't be able to determine many digits of this number.

00:24:33.783 --> 00:24:39.577
We need to multiply by ENk again.

00:24:39.577 --> 00:24:41.617
Down it goes.

00:24:41.617 --> 00:24:45.187
I'll write a 5 here.

00:24:45.187 --> 00:24:46.570
So...

00:24:50.753 --> 00:24:52.328
And if it's 7...

00:24:52.328 --> 00:24:54.131
Oh! It is an overshoot.

00:24:54.131 --> 00:24:56.564
And if it's 6...

00:24:56.564 --> 00:24:57.239
It is an undershoot.

00:24:57.239 --> 00:24:58.257
Alright.

00:24:58.257 --> 00:24:59.702
5.

00:24:59.702 --> 00:25:02.769
5 is an overshoot.

00:25:02.769 --> 00:25:03.958
3.

00:25:03.958 --> 00:25:05.958
2.

00:25:14.154 --> 00:25:26.261
Here, we need to subtract 0.04 for convenience.

00:25:26.261 --> 00:25:27.836
There!

00:25:27.836 --> 00:25:30.726
Oh, by the way, it's even clear that we should continue here.

00:25:30.726 --> 00:25:38.318
We have 0.04, and it suggests we should write another 1 and many sixes.

00:25:38.318 --> 00:25:41.127
Oh! Then it goes straight from zero.

00:25:41.127 --> 00:25:44.408
And now, it will probably be easier here.

00:25:44.408 --> 00:25:47.085
4. Undershoot.

00:25:47.085 --> 00:25:49.335
5. Overshoot.

00:25:49.335 --> 00:25:53.762
So, 4 and 5...

00:25:53.762 --> 00:25:55.762
7...

00:26:06.775 --> 00:26:10.000
Something interesting is starting. Can you see it?

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.338
5...

00:26:12.338 --> 00:26:14.765
6...

00:26:14.765 --> 00:26:16.253
55...

00:26:16.253 --> 00:26:17.863
Wow...

00:26:26.657 --> 00:26:30.845
So, we've reached something. Something very fascinating.

00:26:30.845 --> 00:26:38.309
What is 0.04166666? It's some fractional number.

00:26:38.309 --> 00:26:39.619
Clearly defined.

00:26:39.619 --> 00:26:47.461
To understand what this number is, we can call ContinuedFraction and take a look.

00:26:47.461 --> 00:26:52.435
It's 1/24.

00:26:52.435 --> 00:26:54.435
We can write it like this...

00:26:55.673 --> 00:26:59.422
FromContinuedFraction.

00:26:59.422 --> 00:27:00.474
1/24.

00:27:00.474 --> 00:27:06.865
Here we first converted it into ContinuedFraction and then derived it back.

00:27:06.865 --> 00:27:09.394
How does it work?

00:27:09.394 --> 00:27:12.371
It works approximately like this.

00:27:12.371 --> 00:27:16.961
Suppose you have some number, some unusual number...

00:27:16.961 --> 00:27:19.297
You get the ContinuedFraction.

00:27:19.297 --> 00:27:26.840
But you can see that this number is very large.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:33.643
So, it makes sense to truncate the ContinuedFraction series here

00:27:33.643 --> 00:27:39.351
and write FromContinuedFraction for this set.

00:27:39.351 --> 00:27:44.546
And understand that the nearest fractional number to this is 1/24.

00:27:44.546 --> 00:27:48.581
Of course, you can take everything and get a completely precise description of this number,

00:27:48.581 --> 00:27:55.228
but if you want to get an approximate fractional approximation, do the following:

00:27:55.228 --> 00:28:04.394
Take the ContinuedFraction expansion, truncate it somewhere, and perform the reverse transformation.

00:28:04.394 --> 00:28:10.273
You can find more information about ContinuedFraction online.

00:28:10.273 --> 00:28:18.264
Here's the exact answer, 1/24, if there are an infinite number of sixes.

00:28:18.264 --> 00:28:21.240
So, this is what we have.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:24.899
Let me draw it again.

00:28:24.899 --> 00:28:30.180
Here, it's already evident that Mathematica is reaching its own precision limit in calculations.

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:36.965
The number we got here is the value of the zeta function at -1/2.

00:28:36.965 --> 00:28:40.359
I'm giving it to you as a mere designation.

00:28:40.359 --> 00:28:46.707
Let's say we denote the number that emerged here as zeta of -0,5.

00:28:46.707 --> 00:28:53.440
We currently have no proof that this is precisely the zeta function at this point.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:59.154
For now, we're simply discovering unusual numbers and assigning names to them,

00:28:59.154 --> 00:29:05.069
and Mathematica already provides the ready answer, which I occasionally guide them to.

00:29:05.069 --> 00:29:14.854
This unusual number we discovered here is indeed the value of the zeta function at -1/2.

00:29:14.854 --> 00:29:23.684
Here, we summed the roots, but we can also sum the reciprocals of the roots.

00:29:23.684 --> 00:29:26.639
Oops. Well, here's the answer to the question again.

00:29:26.639 --> 00:29:35.762
You can trust me that in a completely analogous manner, you can get the signs of this number.

00:29:35.762 --> 00:29:53.707
Now, the computation and caching of the value of EDNk at k = -1/2 are taking place.

00:29:53.707 --> 00:30:08.812
And we immediately encounter an unusual number that cannot be simply represented as a fraction.

00:30:18.545 --> 00:30:21.514
Here you need to act as follows:

00:30:21.514 --> 00:30:28.978
Take and multiply by this

00:30:28.978 --> 00:30:33.320
and then start adjusting.

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:36.601
5. Undershoot.

00:30:36.601 --> 00:30:38.743
6. Undershoot.

00:30:38.743 --> 00:30:40.000
7. Overshot.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.122
Then it is 6...

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:49.705
With these manipulations, you draw all these signs.

00:30:49.705 --> 00:30:55.142
And, by the way, it's immediately evident here that you need to subtract 1/2 because the plot starts there.

00:30:55.142 --> 00:30:56.837
It's clear, from 1/2.

00:30:56.837 --> 00:30:59.130
That's how this 1/2 appears.

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:08.381
Then you can multiply again, and here we discover that we need to multiply not by the root, but directly by "m."

00:31:08.381 --> 00:31:12.771
And then we find that we need to add 1/24.

00:31:12.771 --> 00:31:17.326
And, in secret, I'll tell you that this number we encountered here,

00:31:17.326 --> 00:31:20.479
is the value of the zeta function at 1/2.

00:31:20.479 --> 00:31:23.589
What we got here, Mathematica knows, of course.

00:31:23.589 --> 00:31:26.778
And to ask it the answer to the question,

00:31:26.778 --> 00:31:40.517
you have to ask it to output the series for HarmonicNumber at k = 1/2 around "m" equal to infinity.

00:31:40.517 --> 00:31:46.946
Those who studied first-year calculus can guess and understand what is written here, what Mathematica outputted.

00:31:46.946 --> 00:31:54.831
Mathematica provided the expansion of this function in powers of 1/m.

00:31:54.831 --> 00:32:01.606
It still has this term "m."

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:10.171
Initially, it was "m," but I don't want to see all these divisions by 2,

00:32:10.171 --> 00:32:15.202
so I at once indicate the square here, and the expression becomes simpler.

00:32:15.202 --> 00:32:17.580
Not as large and more concise.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:22.050
And, by the way, here, when we take any number,

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:24.300
for example, this,

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.035
and we call FactorInteger,

00:32:27.035 --> 00:32:33.767
we see that it contains 18 twos, so it's (2^18) * (3^1).

00:32:33.767 --> 00:32:35.278
A lot of twos.

00:32:35.278 --> 00:32:43.718
To eliminate these twos, we can choose to divide by 4 here,

00:32:43.718 --> 00:32:57.671
so that it expands into a series for this variable, and then the powers of two stop accumulating in the denominator.

00:33:00.213 --> 00:33:08.018
Mathematica knows perfectly well that in the expansion of HarmonicNumber around infinity,

00:33:08.018 --> 00:33:14.884
the zeta function is present as a constant term.

00:33:14.884 --> 00:33:21.659
Now, this is definitely an interesting phenomenon.

00:33:21.659 --> 00:33:31.251
If you're curious about what happens when you sum the cube roots,

00:33:31.251 --> 00:33:36.069
here's the answer to that question.

00:33:40.171 --> 00:33:48.875
Similarly, you can get the value of the zeta function at 3/2 and -3/2.

00:33:48.875 --> 00:33:53.183
I'd like to draw your attention to the fact

00:33:53.183 --> 00:34:01.586
that these coefficients in these expansions resemble each other.

00:34:01.586 --> 00:34:07.385
Let's, for example, divide 1/18 by 1/168,

00:34:07.385 --> 00:34:12.346
and divide 1/20 by 3/80.

00:34:12.346 --> 00:34:16.779
Let's see what happens when we divide 1/18 by 1/168.

00:34:16.779 --> 00:34:19.249
And what happens when we divide this coefficient

00:34:19.249 --> 00:34:22.131
by this coefficient.

00:34:22.131 --> 00:34:29.423
The ratio of these two coefficients with large numbers in the numerator and denominator gives a simple expression.

00:34:29.423 --> 00:34:31.671
Let's try it out.

00:34:31.671 --> 00:34:43.688
Discover another connection between the expansion of HarmonicNumber at -3/2 and at -1/2.

00:34:43.688 --> 00:34:44.648
So...

00:34:44.648 --> 00:34:49.180
These are the coefficients at 1/2,

00:34:49.180 --> 00:34:53.452
these are the coefficients at -1/2,

00:34:53.452 --> 00:34:55.367
and these are the coefficients at -3/2.

00:34:55.367 --> 00:34:57.446
This is our 1/18,

00:34:57.446 --> 00:34:59.787
and here's 1/168.

00:34:59.787 --> 00:35:03.751
And now let's directly divide a1 by a2.

00:35:03.751 --> 00:35:07.299
For example, a1/a2.

00:35:07.299 --> 00:35:12.849
It's complaining a lot here. To avoid that, we need to disable these warnings.

00:35:12.849 --> 00:35:18.440
And for aesthetics, we need to replace Indeterminate with 0.

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:22.739
And here's what we get:

00:35:26.239 --> 00:35:27.824
76/3

00:35:27.824 --> 00:35:31.745
-20, which is actually -60/3.

00:35:31.745 --> 00:35:33.034
-44/3

00:35:33.034 --> 00:35:34.729
-28/3

00:35:34.729 --> 00:35:37.625
Well, of course, we need to multiply by 3.

00:35:37.625 --> 00:35:42.818
All the numbers we see here are divisible by 4, so it makes sense to divide by 4.

00:35:42.818 --> 00:35:44.719
There you go.

00:35:44.719 --> 00:35:49.615
From this, we can see that the coefficients in the expansion

00:35:49.615 --> 00:35:55.500
this one and this one are connected in a clear way.

00:35:55.500 --> 00:36:00.000
We have discovered this, but we haven't proven it.

00:36:05.848 --> 00:36:12.018
At -1/2, the expansion is connected to the expansion at 1/2.

00:36:12.018 --> 00:36:18.746
Here, we also get ratios with a visible dependence.

00:36:18.746 --> 00:36:24.590
-1, -5, -4, -13, -17... Each number differs from the previous one by 4.

00:36:24.590 --> 00:36:28.163
And all of this can be written out in the form of formulas.

00:36:28.163 --> 00:36:32.291
There's another interesting case when we sum the harmonic series.

00:36:32.291 --> 00:36:35.723
When we sum the reciprocal of natural numbers.

00:36:35.723 --> 00:36:42.873
This corresponds to our K = -1.

00:36:42.873 --> 00:36:54.761
And here, if you try to figure out what needs to be subtracted from this sum,

00:36:54.761 --> 00:36:59.365
after some time, you will realize that it needs to be subtracted by the logarithm.

00:36:59.365 --> 00:37:03.588
But when you subtract the logarithm,

00:37:03.588 --> 00:37:11.644
you will see that it all tends to a certain constant that can be gradually written out.

00:37:27.225 --> 00:37:28.528
And so on.

00:37:28.528 --> 00:37:36.803
Here, an interesting constant is written out, which in Mathematica is called EulerGamma.

00:37:39.791 --> 00:37:42.965
This is what it equals.

00:37:42.965 --> 00:37:46.368
If we write it here

00:37:46.368 --> 00:37:48.897
with a large number of digits, this is what we'll see.

00:37:48.897 --> 00:37:51.099
And it can be seen here that we need to subtract 1/2.

00:37:51.099 --> 00:37:57.410
Again, 1/2. The story of subtraction always starts with 1/2.

00:37:57.410 --> 00:38:02.336
Enclose it in parentheses again.

00:38:02.336 --> 00:38:06.184
Multiply by it.

00:38:06.184 --> 00:38:12.332
Here, perhaps, we need to continue...

00:38:12.332 --> 00:38:18.552
Let me write "EulerGamma" right here.

00:38:18.552 --> 00:38:20.171
Like this...

00:38:20.171 --> 00:38:27.864
+ 0,08

00:38:31.791 --> 00:38:36.793
We also need to add 0.003.

00:38:38.799 --> 00:38:42.216
Alright... I've figured it out.

00:38:48.534 --> 00:38:54.066
It's 1/12.

00:38:54.066 --> 00:39:06.799
These calculations can be continued to get the expansion for the sum of the harmonic series up to the term "m".

00:39:06.799 --> 00:39:09.246
Let's take a look at the answer.

00:39:09.246 --> 00:39:12.734
Here's the representation of this expansion.

00:39:12.734 --> 00:39:15.539
If we continue searching.

00:39:15.539 --> 00:39:24.747
Again, we enclose it in parentheses and multiply again...

00:39:27.564 --> 00:39:31.622
Here, perhaps, we need to square it.

00:39:31.622 --> 00:39:33.735
Here's another number.

00:39:33.735 --> 00:39:38.120
Here we can guess the next elements...

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:43.849
Well, the complete answer, if we keep going, looks like this.

00:39:43.849 --> 00:39:47.730
It's clear that these terms will be difficult to find,

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:54.767
but the thing is that these coefficients are well-known to us.

00:39:54.767 --> 00:40:02.456
Let's get them explicitly using the CoefficientList function.

00:40:02.456 --> 00:40:08.200
But before calling the CoefficientList function, here I used rules

00:40:08.200 --> 00:40:12.926
to get rid of this part and turned 1/m into m.

00:40:12.926 --> 00:40:18.741
The coefficients in this expansion coincide with those very numbers that we got

00:40:18.741 --> 00:40:25.814
in the first column of the inverse matrix of Pascal's triangle,

00:40:25.814 --> 00:40:33.748
and after dividing by the binomial coefficients.

00:40:33.748 --> 00:40:37.944
Their ratio is always equal to one, except for one place where it is -1.

00:40:37.944 --> 00:40:43.359
Again, 1/2 changes its sign.

00:40:43.359 --> 00:40:47.712
So, what did we get?

00:40:47.712 --> 00:40:52.879
We got the following expansion:

00:40:52.879 --> 00:41:05.465
That the sum of the reciprocals of natural numbers up to "m" is equal to the logarithm plus the Euler constant (EulerGamma),

00:41:05.465 --> 00:41:16.978
and then follows the sum of the values of the zeta function at negative points, divided by "m" to the power of some "k".

00:41:16.978 --> 00:41:20.000
Here, I adjusted...

00:41:22.647 --> 00:41:27.314
It's more accurate to write +1. Here.

00:41:27.314 --> 00:41:32.240
This is what we got.

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:44.527
By definition, EulerGamma is a constant equal to the limit of the sum of the harmonic series minus the logarithm of "m".

00:41:44.527 --> 00:41:52.958
As "m" approaches infinity, so it's natural to see it here.

00:41:52.958 --> 00:41:58.126
This component is interesting.

00:41:58.126 --> 00:42:04.990
Let's see what we get here.

00:42:07.039 --> 00:42:12.995
Well, here it is. I just made sure that I got what I needed.

00:42:14.702 --> 00:42:15.735
Alright...

00:42:17.997 --> 00:42:24.919
Here, I calculated the sum of the harmonic series up to term "m" with decimal places,

00:42:24.919 --> 00:42:27.924
and on the other side, I calculated this sum.

00:42:27.924 --> 00:42:30.351
You can see that the numbers coincide. Good.

00:42:30.351 --> 00:42:35.524
We can put a minus sign between them instead of a comma.

00:42:35.524 --> 00:42:37.603
Here, I chose m = 200,

00:42:37.603 --> 00:42:41.012
and here, I chose the sum up to 10.

00:42:41.012 --> 00:42:43.647
Like this...

00:42:46.464 --> 00:42:49.531
And if we put 1000 here, I'm afraid it won't be pleasant.

00:42:49.531 --> 00:42:52.806
Yes, an unpleasant thing happened...

00:42:52.806 --> 00:42:55.739
Alright, just a moment...

00:42:55.739 --> 00:43:00.892
This is all very interesting, of course.

00:43:00.892 --> 00:43:05.703
On the one hand, you can see that there is some convergence,

00:43:05.703 --> 00:43:12.350
but it is not recommended to increase the sum here to a thousand.

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:15.558
Well... That's a separate topic. A very complex topic.

00:43:15.558 --> 00:43:17.217
Alright.

00:43:17.217 --> 00:43:27.566
I just want to explain the meaning of a quite complex and interesting equation.

00:43:29.743 --> 00:43:34.249
The meaning is... Let me draw it from the top here.

00:43:34.249 --> 00:43:40.000
Here, the function 1/x is drawn, it's the red line.

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:42.788
It's drawn here twice. First at the beginning, then at the end.

00:43:42.788 --> 00:43:48.417
In order for this area to be colored in red.

00:43:48.417 --> 00:43:56.728
Then there is this step function up to the 10th element.

00:43:56.728 --> 00:44:02.929
And then it continues with a different color.

00:44:02.929 --> 00:44:08.429
Overall, this preparation is done.

00:44:08.429 --> 00:44:13.012
The area under the smooth curve is the logarithm.

00:44:13.012 --> 00:44:16.842
When we stop at 10 and calculate the area under this smooth curve,

00:44:16.842 --> 00:44:22.019
it is exactly the natural logarithm of 10.

00:44:22.019 --> 00:44:24.929
The sum of these triangles...

00:44:24.929 --> 00:44:31.667
all of them, an infinite number of triangles. They are not perfect triangles, one side is smooth.

00:44:31.667 --> 00:44:41.260
So, the sum of them equals EulerGamma, an unusual number that we calculated earlier.

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:45.559
It's approximately 0.5, but a bit larger, 0.57.

00:44:45.559 --> 00:44:53.230
By the way, if these triangles were perfect and there was a straight line segment here,

00:44:53.230 --> 00:44:59.029
then the sum of these triangles would exactly be 0.5.

00:44:59.029 --> 00:44:59.739
Why is that?

00:44:59.739 --> 00:45:04.252
Because the triangles can be aligned to the left edge

00:45:04.252 --> 00:45:08.941
and it can be determined that they exactly occupy half of this column,

00:45:08.941 --> 00:45:12.813
if they have this segment as a straight line.

00:45:12.813 --> 00:45:19.423
But they don't have this segment as a straight line, so it's slightly larger than half.

00:45:19.423 --> 00:45:23.985
So, the sum of all the triangles is EulerGamma,

00:45:23.985 --> 00:45:26.961
this number right here.

00:45:29.351 --> 00:45:35.334
It turns out that this sum should be negative

00:45:35.334 --> 00:45:38.487
and correspond to subtracting all these little triangles

00:45:38.487 --> 00:45:39.761
that we don't want to calculate,

00:45:39.761 --> 00:45:46.670
because we only want to calculate the sum of the harmonic series up to the 10th term.

00:45:47.737 --> 00:45:50.489
That's the meaning of it.

00:45:50.489 --> 00:45:56.239
This sum is negative

00:45:56.239 --> 00:46:04.685
and equals the sum of triangles starting from the m-th triangle.

00:46:07.033 --> 00:46:14.451
These triangles can be separately drawn using the RegionPlot function.

00:46:14.451 --> 00:46:17.982
Like this.

00:46:17.982 --> 00:46:29.605
And here below are four triangles aligned to the left edge, as I mentioned.

00:46:29.605 --> 00:46:36.414
It takes a long time because I asked RegionPlot to draw it all neatly,

00:46:36.414 --> 00:46:44.006
using a large number of calculations.

00:46:44.006 --> 00:46:49.202
So, we got all these triangles separately from everything.

00:46:49.202 --> 00:46:58.538
It says: "y" is greater than 1/x, and at the same time "y" is less than 1 divided by the integer part of "x".

00:46:58.538 --> 00:47:05.793
And "x" is greater than 1 so that it doesn't draw anything from 0 to 1.

00:47:05.793 --> 00:47:09.691
To align them to the left...

00:47:09.691 --> 00:47:10.746
It's not that easy to do,

00:47:10.746 --> 00:47:12.484
but, for example, like this.

00:47:12.484 --> 00:47:16.600
We draw four different regions,

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:18.637
Mathematica assigns them four different colors.

00:47:18.637 --> 00:47:21.826
If desired, the number of "regions" can be increased.

00:47:21.826 --> 00:47:26.254
Here, I simply shifted each piece to the left.

00:47:26.254 --> 00:47:30.542
First by 1, then by 2, then by 3, then by 4.

00:47:34.427 --> 00:47:37.398
So, what have we done?

00:47:37.398 --> 00:47:43.020
We recalled the previous video where we got this matrix

00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:47.234
by inverting this matrix.

00:47:47.234 --> 00:47:51.847
We also remembered that we found a pattern in this matrix

00:47:51.847 --> 00:48:00.000
and divided the elements of the inverted matrix by the elements of the original matrix, but slightly shifted,

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.817
in other words, we divided them by the binomial coefficients, but slightly shifted.

00:48:04.817 --> 00:48:11.257
And we got diagonals consisting of the same numbers, as shown in the previous video.

00:48:11.257 --> 00:48:18.721
So we saw that there are certain numbers hidden in the idea of Pascal's Triangle inversion,

00:48:18.721 --> 00:48:21.745
a sequence of numbers.

00:48:21.745 --> 00:48:26.349
And the entire inverse matrix can be got from these numbers.

00:48:26.349 --> 00:48:30.825
We called these numbers zeta minus 1, zeta minus 2, zeta minus 3, and so on.

00:48:30.825 --> 00:48:38.923
And it turned out that these numbers, which are in the first column, play an important role.

00:48:38.923 --> 00:48:41.124
They appear everywhere.

00:48:41.124 --> 00:48:48.795
And, in particular, they appear when calculating the partial sum of the harmonic series.

00:48:48.795 --> 00:48:56.093
We got a beautiful, complex formula.

00:48:56.093 --> 00:48:59.117
We sort of discovered that it's true.

00:48:59.117 --> 00:49:02.520
We also discovered that it behaves strangely.

00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:07.807
Actually, writing infinity here is probably not possible. We have to write some small numbers.

00:49:07.807 --> 00:49:11.600
And it provides a fairly good approximation.

00:49:16.722 --> 00:49:19.525
And this is already quite serious mathematics.

00:49:19.525 --> 00:49:21.305
That's all for today!

00:49:21.305 --> 00:49:23.476
Good luck, everyone! Bye!

